n docs/deploy.md.\n\nThe gap in 94852194 is closed:\n1. Only manifest references of kind rules get a summary. Every other kind is recorded {scope, status: \"not-applicable\"} in a third array, with no request and no error. The note and docs/deploy.md state that this trusts the validated manifest for kind, as it already trusts it for which scopes exist, and that a skipped kind is not a fresh observation. The new array is counted toward the 16 MiB output cap like the others.\n2. Native witness, on the real path. It builds a room with all seven kinds (register, directory, membership, rules, destination, two inboxes, lane) and installs one Counter application through the real runtime. It then runs the command's actual read loop under life.owner.required.\n   - Member B joins through real Membership and signs no Rules entry.\n   - Without configured membership, B's key makes one Signed Rules GET, which answers forbidden: exit 1, zero applications, one error.\n   - With membership configured, B gets a session from the real issuer: one POST and one Rules GET. The result is exit 0, one row (the lane scope, the install item, the exact release, the publisher and host git.example), no errors and seven not-applicable entries.\n   - The token, the invitation secret and the release URI are absent from the output. A separate real register summary with that session answers forbidden, which confirms the source finding.\n   - The TEST fetch bridge changes only redirect \"error\" to \"manual\" for Workerd, after asserting the command's options. Method, body, authorization and signal are unchanged, and so is the production wrapper. The two failed Native attempts that found that seam are kept as failures.\n3. Controls (raw output read). Each mutant typechecks, fails exactly one named case (1 failed, 8 skipped), and restores adapter SHA-256 11996e1c, which equals the head's bytes:\n   - reading non-Rules kinds fails the session-pair case;\n   - passing a null reader fails the same case;\n   - continuing after an uncertain acquisition fails the stop case;\n   - pushing skipped rows without byte accounting fails the output-bound case.\n4. The coverage correction 578b5da4 is now in docs/deploy.md. The report covers only the repository rooted at the configured Directory. Before an R11 deploy, the planner must record a separately authorized coverage path for other repositories, or an explicit decision to withdraw installations outside the trust list.\n\nValidation:\n- The new ordinary gate at ecb6d08d (tree 1169aad0) passed: clean before and after, no signal, Node 66, Counting 55, plain 2,863 passed and 1 skipped, Native 796 passed and 2 skipped (the new case included), 0 unhandled. The note gives 932.4 s wall and 1,981.1 s child CPU (one run). The earlier gate at 30a0933d stays a result for the rejected head only.\n- After the gate only docs/deploy.md, the note and the manifest change, all prose, so the documents-only rule applies.\n- The manifest verifies against the sealed recut directory: 171 files, 0 bad, nothing writable, SHA256SUMS all OK. It includes an exact copy of the rejected head's 73 files.\n- check-notes 6f47bf98..776f810a reports 0 warnings, and git diff --check is clean.\n- check-doc-links on an exported tree of the head: 712 relative links checked, 0 broken.\n\nArchitecture: sound. One guard on the kind, one more array, and a session acquisition reused from the existing client. It adds no server path, setting or authority.\n\nSecurity: no finding. Skipping non-Rules kinds cannot hide an installation, because only native Rules carry installation items. The session token never reaches disk or output; the Native witness checks that.\n\nSimplification: acceptable. Nit, not blocking: a manifest with no Rules reference reports complete after zero reads. Every repository has a Rules scope, so this cannot arise from a real inventory; a fixed error for it would make that explicit.\n\nVerdict: approved at exact head 776f810a. The builder lands. 